

SPINN was intended to be a "autofitting program" that uses machine learning scripts , learns how to spectral analyze and basically does all the research for you, e.g. plot spectra, compare spectra wil model spectra, begin assigning, good fit, continue, bad fit try again. 
So basically it does a spectral analysis like us humans (lol) and then moves to fit the way we do, e.g. trial and error. its a lot to explain and i don't want to  if its all for nothing. Anyways, if a fit of less than 100 kHz is reached, then it changes your input file, then it continues. 
If not reached,  it restarts and continues trying. 
Once completed it can produce the fitted constants, analyze those constants, references checks against the model inputed constants , e.g. rotational constants, hyperfine, fine, etc. you put in the input file, all the fun stuff we do to prepare for our paper writing. 
If quadXX is included it will perform a Townes-Dailey analysis and generates a table for you to take fitted constants with their estimated uncertainties. 


